[goer@quads.uchicago.edu (Richard L. Goerwitz) writes:]
[] Of course, no one expects NetHack to be a gem of historical consistency.
[] This is absurd.  I guess I'd just like it if, when it dips into the
[] old lore, NetHack would be just a *tad* less cavalier with it.

to the extent possible NetHack should be accurate.  however, it is
far more important, to me, that NetHack be decipherable to the alert
player with no other resources at her disposal except the game and
perhaps the manual which accompanies it.  however lore is handled,
it ought to be self-consistent in the NetHack world (i.e. clues as
to what was on the mind of the developers should be attainable so
the user can understand the game).  perhaps, in NetHack, there ought
to be a policy that every "obscure" feature should be accompanied by
at least 4 rumors and possibly another source of info that can shed
light on it (without giving it all away).

it's icing on the cake when an alert player can turn to references
outside of NetHack and make sense of the game (and the necessity of
that should be minimized).

